# Copy to .env (gitignored) and edit. `pulse.sh` auto-sources .env.

# Directory tree of YYYY/MM/DD/*.md notes (e.g. an Obsidian vault that
# stores exported LLM chats). Required.
PI_PULSE_NOTES_DIR="$HOME/notes/chats"

# Where to drop the daily brief in addition to ./out/. Optional. If
# unset, the brief is only written to ./out/YYYY-MM-DD.md.
PI_PULSE_DELIVERY="$HOME/notes/Pulse"

# Path to the anki_search.py script from the anki-search skill.
# Optional; if unset, the collector looks at the default location
# ~/.claude/skills/anki-search/anki_search.py and on $PATH.
# PI_PULSE_ANKI_SEARCH="$HOME/.claude/skills/anki-search/anki_search.py"

# Brave Search API key. REQUIRED for scout and for expand's deterministic
# fallback search. The in-repo broker reads this value directly from .env;
# pulse.sh deliberately does not export it into any Pi model process.
# Get a Brave Search API subscription at
# https://api-dashboard.search.brave.com/register and paste the key here.
#
# IMPORTANT for launchd / cron: env vars from ~/.zprofile, ~/.profile,
# or ~/.zshrc are NOT inherited by launchd jobs. Setting BRAVE_API_KEY
# only in your shell profile is intentionally insufficient: put the key
# HERE so the broker can read it under both interactive and scheduled runs.
# The launchd plist itself does NOT need to declare it.
# BRAVE_API_KEY="brv-..."

# Pi provider + model. The current default runs every stage on
# glm-5.2:cloud (top open-weight model as of June 2026), configured under
# an `ollama` provider in the repo-owned catalog
# pi-agent/models.json.template -- NOT ~/.pi/agent/models.json, which
# `ollama launch pi` rewrites without a thinkingLevelMap or contextWindow.
# pulse.sh renders the template to .pi-agent/ and points Pi there.
PI_PROVIDER=ollama
PI_MODEL=glm-5.2:cloud

# Where the Ollama OpenAI-compat endpoint lives; substituted into the
# rendered catalog. Use the host's LAN/tailnet address if Ollama runs on
# another machine.
PI_PULSE_OLLAMA_BASE_URL=http://localhost:11434/v1

# Per-stage model/provider/thinking overrides. Each falls back to the
# global PI_PROVIDER/PI_MODEL above, so leaving them unset reproduces the
# single-model default. <STAGE> is DISTILL, SCOUT, PLAN, or EXPAND.
#
# glm-5.2 is a reasoning model and on a heavy synthesis turn can
# intermittently (~20%) spend the whole turn on chain-of-thought and emit no
# answer text -> the stage writes 0 bytes and pulse.sh aborts. This is handled
# by running scout with thinking OFF (stall-proof; see below) and by wrapping
# distill/scout/plan in run_pi_retry (resample on 0-byte output). See CLAUDE.md
# "glm-5.2 thinking-runaway and the scout fix".
#
# Historical alternative: before glm-5.2, kimi-k2.6:cloud (which saturated the
# synthesis prompts the same way) ran scout+expand while minimax-m3:cloud (a
# non-reasoning model with a larger output budget) ran distill+plan:
# PI_PULSE_DISTILL_MODEL=minimax-m3:cloud
# PI_PULSE_PLAN_MODEL=minimax-m3:cloud
#
# Note on PI_PULSE_<STAGE>_THINKING + Ollama: pi drives Ollama over the
# OpenAI-compat endpoint and sends the level as reasoning_effort, which Ollama
# accepts only as high|medium|low|max|none. pi's bare labels do NOT line up:
# "off" is omitted (model still thinks), "xhigh" clamps to "high", and
# "minimal" is sent verbatim and 400s the request (-> 0 bytes -> abort; never
# use it). To reach a true "off" (none) or "max" you must add a thinkingLevelMap
# to the model entry in ~/.pi/agent/models.json, e.g. for glm-5.2:cloud:
#   "thinkingLevelMap": {"off":"none","low":"low","medium":"medium",
#                        "high":"high","xhigh":"max"}
#
# Scout on glm-5.2 is run with thinking OFF: glm-5.2 intermittently (~20%) ends
# its scout synthesis turn inside its reasoning channel with no answer text,
# writing 0 bytes and aborting the run. Disabling thinking makes scout
# stall-proof by construction (and was faster with equal/fuller output in a live
# test). Requires the thinkingLevelMap above. distill/plan keep thinking on but
# are guarded by run_pi_retry (resample on 0-byte; PI_PULSE_SYNTH_RETRIES,
# default 3).
PI_PULSE_SCOUT_THINKING=off
# PI_PULSE_DISTILL_PROVIDER=ollama
# Total attempts per single-shot synthesis stage (distill/scout/plan):
# PI_PULSE_SYNTH_RETRIES=3

# History windows (days). Defaults: 30 notes, 7 sesh.
# PI_PULSE_NOTES_SINCE=30
# PI_PULSE_SESH_SINCE=7

# Card quotas are CAPS, not targets. Plan picks up to this many of
# each type from the scout-discovered signal sheet; on slow signal
# days the brief shrinks rather than padding. Defaults: 5 tracked +
# 2 adjacent + 1 bridge + 1 follow-up (follow-up consumes one tracked
# slot, so the cap is 8 cards total).
#
# - tracked   = signals with relation=memo-anchored
# - adjacent  = signals with relation=profile-adjacent
# - bridge    = signals with relation=study-bridge
# - follow-up = re-coverage of a topic from a recent brief with a
#               fresh signal (consumes one tracked slot)
#
# Set bridge=0 if you don't use Anki or don't want the leech-bridge
# card. Set followup=0 to disable follow-ups entirely.
# PI_PULSE_CARDS_TRACKED=5
# PI_PULSE_CARDS_ADJACENT=2
# PI_PULSE_CARDS_BRIDGE=1
# PI_PULSE_CARDS_FOLLOWUP=1

# Recent-feedback digest cap (daily plan-stage prior). The digest
# (.tmp/feedback_recent.md) is attached to the plan pi call as a
# ranking prior; an uncapped digest (one bulk rating session put ~184
# rows in it) ballooned the plan call's latency and output. This caps
# each of the four rating sections (valued / neutral / not-valued /
# avoid) to its top N rows by rating strength then recency; 0 disables
# the cap. The weekly profile-suggest stage always uses the FULL
# (uncapped) digest regardless of this value. Default 20.
# PI_PULSE_FEEDBACK_DIGEST_MAX=20

# Scout-stage budgets. Scout runs broad brave-search queries per
# interest cluster to discover fresh primary sources before plan
# picks. Tighter caps run faster but find fewer signals.
# PI_PULSE_SCOUT_MAX_INTERESTS=12
# PI_PULSE_SCOUT_QUERIES_PER_INTEREST=2

# Per-slot expand concurrency. Each planned card runs as its own pi
# session in parallel, capped by this. Higher values cut wall time
# but compete for Ollama daemon resources.
# PI_PULSE_EXPAND_PARALLEL=4

# Private run-history retention. Disabled by default: unset/0 preserves every
# transcript, memo, and log. Set a positive day count only if you explicitly
# want date-stamped logs and .pulse-sessions entries pruned after each run.
# PI_PULSE_RETENTION_DAYS=0

# Feedback web server (scripts/feedback-server.sh). Serves the rendered
# briefs with a tap-to-rate widget that writes marks back into
# out/*.feedback.md (same grammar as the TUI; the next pulse.sh run
# ingests them). Host is a bind address: 127.0.0.1 (default) keeps it
# local-only; the keyword `tailscale` autodetects this machine's
# Tailscale IPv4 (via `tailscale ip -4`) so other tailnet devices can
# reach http://<machine-tailnet-name>:8377/ while nothing on the LAN
# can; or set an explicit IP. The server also refuses any client that
# is not loopback or a Tailscale address, regardless of binding.
# PI_PULSE_FEEDBACK_HOST=127.0.0.1
# PI_PULSE_FEEDBACK_PORT=8377
#
# The rating API only accepts browser POSTs whose Origin names this
# server's own identity: the bind host, loopback aliases when bound to
# loopback, and (with `tailscale`) the machine's autodetected MagicDNS
# name. To rate from a browser using any other hostname for this
# machine, opt that name in here (comma-separated).
# PI_PULSE_FEEDBACK_ALLOWED_HOSTS=
